it has been a hundred years since the  end of World War one the Armistice  signed in 1918 was supposed to bring the  world together but a group of American  soldiers who were fighting for freedom  and democracy had yet to experience  those ideals in their own country on the  fourth floor of the new Smithsonian  Museum so just how segregated was a  military it was completely segregated is  a tribute to American veterans the  freedmen of the Massachusetts 54th the  Buffalo Soldiers the Tuskegee Airmen and  a regiment with an unforgettable  nickname do we know why these guys were  called the Harlem Hellfighters I really  don't think we do it whether it was the  French whether it was the Germans or  whether it was the American press crew  Walski Salter is a guest curator at the  Museum of african-american history and  culture the Harlem Hellfighters is a  name no matter where the origin came  from sticks to the heart of the way  these men served and fought a band of  brothers who faced adversity from the  start  where are they sent to be trained in  Spartanburg South Carolina you have  these northern African Americans going  down in the heart of this era where  there are these riots clashes between  white Americans and African Americans  among the first American troops to  arrive in France their first few months  weren't spent fighting digging ditches  digging latrine digging latrines making  roads building camps why well there are  many reasons but I think a part of it  there was a political pool and then  there was this issue some of the white  Americans did not want African American  units to fight but the European allies  were desperate for fighters so  commanding general john pershing handed  over the all-black unit to the French  the 369th served 191 days in combat more  than any other American troops the germ  saw that these men fought ferociously on  the battlefield some weren't just  fighting Lieutenant James Europe led the  Hellfighters regiment band the Greek was  credited with bringing jazz to the  French every regiment had a band but  there was no band like a 360 night band  and they combined this new music that  was heard from the very time they set  foot off the ship and they were sent on  a tour around France but the battlefield  was where they made their name their  most famous act of heroism happened in  May of 1918 privates Henry Johnson and  Needham robbers were sentries at an  isolated outpost when they were attacked  by at least a dozen Germans and so  during that fight while Johnson runs out  of bullets  he's throwing grenades he's using his  rifle as a club and he ends up using his  bolo knife he also prevents Germans from  dragging his buddy  Needham Roberts away Johnson and Roberts  would become the first American soldiers  of any race to receive the krodha care  when of Francis highest awards for valor  that was the night that the bravery the  patriotism and the service of what  African Americans were capable of and  willing to do for their country became  known throughout the world more than a  hundred and fifty other Hellfighters  were given the Croix de Guerre including  Lawrence McVeigh senior this one is his  military uniform he looks so proud he  does Geena McVeigh is the corporals  granddaughter when did you discover the  true story about 2004-5 I went to the  car dealership to get my car repaired it  was a gentleman in uniform sitting next  to me waiting for his car so I thanked  him for his service and I said oh my  grandfather fought in the war he goes he  did I said yeah well one  and I said he won this French meadow he  said what I said he won a French meadow  he goes well can I ask you a question I  said yes goes was your grandfather a  black man I said yes he said was the  meadow the quad the gear I said yes he  said do you know what you have I'm like  a metal he said no you have history  McVeigh contacted the Smithsonian and  was asked to bring in her grandfather's  belongings I said it's all yours  and they they're like really I said yeah  I donated a haul despite enduring more  days in combat than any other American  troops despite being the first Allies to  reach the Rhine River despite no member  ever being taken prisoner the American  government refused them an important  honor they were not allowed to march in  the victory parade US government said no  if they can't March this was in France  but in February of 1919 the hell  fighters were allowed to parade up New  York City's Fifth Avenue to Harlem you  see the pride of these men and when you  watch that formation you know what's  there an even greater sense of pride  came in 2015 when one of the hell  fighters received the ultimate award  from America's first black president the  President of the United States of  America has awarded in the name of  Congress the Medal of Honor to private  Henry Johnson United States Army it's  just amazing that all the things that  the African American community has done  with the weight of not being good enough  on their back they didn't let that stop  them they went ahead and they fought for  what they thought was right  what's great about the herstory is she  was just thanking a veteran for his  service we got so much more and it was a  white veteran yeah  which goes to the point I want to give a  shout out to Colonel William Hayward he  was the officer over the 369th this  all-black unit he gave them respect  he gave them honor and what he did was  bring attention to them he's one of the  reasons why they were able to  at that parade up to Harlem well and why  many people believe the Croix de Guerre  was bestowed upon them and I love what  Gina McVeigh said with it it said do you  know what you have you have  [Laughter]
 

